    id: "420dcbd51301ca2f0ea6",
    cat: "conflitti",
    categories: ["conflitti"],
    title: "Ukraine says bodycam video shows deadly Russian double-tap bombing",
    summary: "At least 40 people were killed and another 30 may still be under the rubble after Monday's attack on a Ukrainian village, Kyiv says.",
    preview: "A bodycam video has been released by Ukraine police which they say shows the moments between two powerful Russian bomb strikes that killed at least 40 people at an aid centre and a shelter. The footage, from a camera on one of the killed police officers, shows residents of the southern village of Kamyshuvakha going to an underground shelter after the first attack on Monday. Soon after a blast is heard and flames are seen as the shelter is hit.",
    body: "A bodycam video has been released by Ukraine police which they say shows the moments between two powerful Russian bomb strikes that killed at least 40 people at an aid centre and a shelter.\n\nThe footage, from a camera on one of the killed police officers, shows residents of the southern village of Kamyshuvakha going to an underground shelter after the first attack on Monday. Soon after a blast is heard and flames are seen as the shelter is hit.\n\nPresident Volodymyr Zelensky said more than 30 people may still be under the rubble, as a search operation continued for the third day.\n\nNine gliding bombs, some weighing 3,000kg, hit the village as people had gathered to get humanitarian aid, Ukraine said.\n\nThe bodycam footage was released by Ukraine's national police on Wednesday.\n\nIt shows huge devastation after the first bomb strike on the village in Ukraine's Zaporizhzhia region, as shocked local residents stare at the rubble.\n\nSeverely damaged buildings are also seen.\n\nA voice of a military official is then heard on a two-way radio warning police on the ground of more glide bombs being launched towards Kamyshuvakha.\n\n\"Quickly [go] to the shelter!\" one of the police officers tells local residents, who are seen walking into the shelter - at least two floors underground.\n\nSeconds later the shelter is hit, and the recording stops.\n\nUkraine said the shelter was near a local school, which was being used to distribute humanitarian aid.\n\nBBC Verify has confirmed the video was filmed in the same place as footage released by the Ukrainian state emergencies service DSNS showing the aftermath Monday's attack in Komyshuvakha.\n\nIn a post on social media on Wednesday, Zelensky said that \"nine aerial bombs were dropped by the Russians.\n\n\"First, they hit people who came to receive humanitarian aid, and then rescuers and police officers who arrived at the scene.\"\n\nThe Ukrainian president added that \"unfortunately, there has been very little reaction from the world to this insane attack\", describing it as yet another of \"thousands\" of war crimes committed by Russia.\n\nUkraine's DSNS earlier posted another video showing a huge crater left after the attack on Kamyshuvakha.\n\nThe Russian military has not publicly commented.\n\nRussian President Vladimir Putin launched a full-scale invasion of Ukraine in February 2022.\n\nIn a separate development on Wednesday, Zelensky said he talked \"in great detail for an hour-and-a-half\" with US President Donald Trump's peace envoys Steve Witkoff and Jared Kushner ahead of trilateral negotiations between Ukraine, Russia and the US on 10-11 November in Abu-Dhabi.\n\nThe Ukrainian leader - who is currently making a surprise visit to Spain - also said his team had a separate \"meaningful\" meeting in Brussels with representatives from the US, the EU, France, Britain, Germany, as well as Nato and the World Bank about \"post-war reconstruction\".\n\nHe added that the meeting in Belgium's capital would continue on Thursday to discuss \"security guarantees\" for Ukraine.\n\nSince the trilateral talks aimed at ending the war were suspended in February, Moscow has stepped up attacks as Kyiv virtually ran out of Western-supplied interceptors to defend against missiles.\n\nAnalysts expect no major breakthrough when the talks resume in the capital of the United Arab Emirates.\n\nAdditional reporting Paul Brown, BBC Verify\n\nRelated topics\n\n- Published2 days ago",
